2. MULTI-TENANT SAAS ARCHITECTURE

Implement a secure multi-tenant architecture.

A tenant represents a paying customer account.

Support two tenant types:

A. COMPANY TENANT

- One company or organisation.
- Its own employees, users, IT department, departments and locations.
- Its own asset register.
- Its own tickets, service requests, maintenance and reports.

B. MSP TENANT

- An IT service provider or managed service provider.
- Can register and manage multiple client organisations.
- Each client organisation has its own users, locations, departments, assets, tickets, contracts and service records.
- MSP administrators can manage all authorised clients.
- MSP technicians can only access client organisations they have been authorised to support.
- Support client-specific permissions, contracts and SLA policies.

Use a tenantId and orgId data model.

tenantId identifies the paying customer account.

orgId identifies the organisation whose records are being managed.

For a direct company, orgId represents the company.

For an MSP, orgId represents one of the MSP's registered client organisations.

All business records must be scoped to the correct tenantId and orgId.

A user may have memberships in multiple tenants. Provide an organisation and tenant switching interface where appropriate.

Do not trust tenantId or orgId supplied by the frontend as proof of access.

Resolve the authenticated user's identity on the backend, load their active membership, verify tenant membership and organisation-level permissions, and authorise every operation.

Implement strict data isolation. No customer must be able to access another customer's assets, tickets, employees, reports, documents or records.

